l,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.018421645634818438,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":69.3,"_gate_insider_own":0.15896,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":1.0,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":100.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"TWFG, Inc. operates an independent distribution platform for personal and commercial insurance products in the United States. The company offers auto, home, renters, life, health, motorcycle, umbrella, boat, recreational vehicle, flood, wind, event, luxury item, general liability, property, business auto, workers' compensation, business owner policy, and professional liability insurance products, as well as commercial bonds and group benefits. TWFG, Inc. was founded in 2001 and is based in The Woodlands, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Richard Finley Bunch III | Chairman & CEO | Age 51 | Compensation: $740,224 (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 400 people. Revenue per employee of $621K ranks in the above the sector median among Financial Services peers. Each employee generates $133K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $11K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity).","financial_summary":["TWFG, Inc. is a small-cap financial services business with a $363M market cap on $249M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +22% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","The business throws off $53M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 21 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 6.72\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","Accruals are elevated at -12.2% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 16% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins 14% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (11.5% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 64% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Valued at a 32% discount to sector m